      </section>
      <section class="faq" id="faq">
        <div class="wrap faq-grid">
          <h2>Data centres and your hydro bill: fair questions</h2>
          <div>
            <details>
              <summary>Won’t data centres raise my hydro bill?</summary>
              <p>They can, if they connect without paying for the new supply they need. In some US states, bills have risen with data centre growth. The energy minister pointed to this in October 2025, when BC debated Bill 31, the law behind the cap. This campaign asks for the opposite: data centres pay for more new generation and transmission than they use, so the grid grows without raising your bill.
                <span class="srcs"><a href="https://lims.leg.bc.ca/hdms/file/Debates/43rd1st/20251023pm-Hansard-n88.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hansard, Oct 23, 2025</a></span></p>
            </details>
            <details>
              <summary>Don’t we normally share the cost of new power?</summary>
              <p>For most customers, yes. The biggest ones already pay more. BC Hydro’s tariff makes a large industrial customer pay for its own line to the grid, and when a new load is over about 150 MW, the upgrades it helps pay for can include new generation. Today’s cap keeps each data centre project at 145 MW, close to that line. Some remote mines build their own power: the Diavik diamond mine in the Northwest Territories added a wind farm that covers part of its needs. This campaign asks data centres to go further at grid scale: pay for more new power than they use.
                <span class="srcs"><a href="https://app.bchydro.com/content/dam/hydro/medialib/internet/documents/appcontent/your_account/Electric_Tariff_Supplement_Number_6.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BC Hydro Tariff Supplement 6</a> <a href={U.reg} target="_blank" rel="noopener">B.C. Reg. 8/2026</a> <a href="https://natural-resources.canada.ca/maps-tools-publications/publications/diavik-diamond-mine-northwest-territories" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Natural Resources Canada</a></span></p>
            </details>
            <details>
              <summary>Isn’t building new power hard?</summary>
              <p>Yes, and slow, but it doesn’t have to mean new dams. BC Hydro’s 2025 call for power picked four wind projects, which come online between 2032 and 2033. Power lines are often the slower part: new transmission can take four to eight years in advanced economies. Under this deal, data centres pay for the lines as well as the power. That’s why the terms should be set now, before data centres connect. First Nations are partners in that work: every project in that call is 51% First Nations-owned. A “pay to connect” rule doesn’t skip any of that work. It pays for more of it.
                <span class="srcs"><a href={U.call} target="_blank" rel="noopener">BC Gov News, May 2026</a> <a href="https://www.iea.org/reports/energy-and-ai/executive-summary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IEA, Energy and AI</a></span></p>
            </details>
            <details>
              <summary>Isn’t AI bad for the environment?</summary>
              <p>It uses a lot of power. Data centres used about 1.5% of the world’s electricity in 2024, and the International Energy Agency expects their electricity use to more than double by 2030. They will be built somewhere either way. About 98% of the electricity BC Hydro generates comes from clean or renewable sources, and under this deal each data centre would also pay for more new clean power than it uses and follow strict water rules. Whatever you think of AI, that’s better than the same computing running on fossil fuels somewhere else.
                <span class="srcs"><a href="https://www.iea.org/reports/energy-and-ai/executive-summary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IEA, Energy and AI</a> <a href={U.shortfall} target="_blank" rel="noopener">BC Gov News, Aug 2026</a></span></p>
            </details>
            <details>
              <summary>Doesn’t BC already have a power shortage?</summary>
              <p>BC is tight on power: demand is growing and the province says BC needs more generating capacity sooner than expected. We need new supply either way. If data centres help pay for it, households pay less of it.
                <span class="srcs"><a href="https://www.bchydro.com/news/press_centre/news_releases/2025/ltep.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BC Hydro</a> <a href={U.shortfall} target="_blank" rel="noopener">BC Gov News, Aug 2026</a></span></p>
            </details>
            <details>
              <summary>What about water?</summary>
              <p>BC has a lot of water, new sealed-loop designs use far less, and BC can set the rules. The facts and sources are in <a href="#water">the water section</a>.</p>
            </details>
            <details>
              <summary>Do data centres create jobs?</summary>
              <p>Yes, and lots of them while they’re built. Meta’s Alberta campus needs more than 3,000 construction workers at its peak, then 300 permanent staff. TELUS expects more than 1,000 construction jobs at its BC sites, plus hundreds of operations roles. The new power adds more: BC Hydro’s 2025 call for power is expected to create 1,500 jobs. Data centres also pay property tax. In Loudoun County, Virginia, the county said in 2025 that data centres generate 38% of its general fund revenue. This campaign asks for no tax breaks.
                <span class="srcs"><a href="https://majorprojects.alberta.ca/details/Meta-Data-Centre/12183" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alberta Major Projects</a> <a href="https://www.telus.com/en/about/news-and-events/media-releases/TELUS-and-Government-of-Canada-advance-work-to-scale-Canadas-sovereign-AI-infrastructure" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TELUS</a> <a href={U.call} target="_blank" rel="noopener">BC Gov News</a> <a href="https://www.loudoun.gov/CivicAlerts.aspx?AID=9635" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Loudoun County</a></span></p>
            </details>
            <details>
              <summary>Why not a moratorium on data centres?</summary>
              <p>A pause doesn’t fix the power BC already needs, and the investment can go to other provinces instead. A “pay to connect” rule does the opposite: a project connects only if it pays for the new supply it needs.
                <span class="srcs"><a href="https://www.bchydro.com/news/press_centre/news_releases/2025/ltep.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BC Hydro</a></span></p>
            </details>
            <details>
              <summary>What if the data centres leave?</summary>
              <p>Long-term contracts with minimum payments mean BC isn’t left paying for half-used lines. AEP Ohio already bills data centres for at least 85% of their highest monthly demand in the past 11 months, even if they use less.
                <span class="srcs"><a href={U.ohio} target="_blank" rel="noopener">AEP Ohio</a></span></p>
            </details>
            <details>
              <summary>What kind of power would they pay for?</summary>
              <p>New clean power, and BC Hydro picks the mix. Its 2025 call for power picked four wind projects, and it calls onshore wind its lowest-cost new energy. It is also adding a sixth generating unit at Revelstoke. Under this deal, data centres pay for more new power than they use.
                <span class="srcs"><a href={U.call} target="_blank" rel="noopener">BC Gov News, May 2026</a> <a href={U.irp} target="_blank" rel="noopener">BC Hydro 2025 plan</a></span></p>
            </details>
            <details>
              <summary>Isn’t this already decided?</summary>
              <p>No. The cap comes from Bill 31 (2025) and the Data Centre Facility and Hydrogen Production Facility Power Supply Regulation (B.C. Reg. 8/2026). That regulation is set by cabinet, so the new government can change it without a new law. BC Hydro has shortlisted some applicants but has not announced which projects get power.
                <span class="srcs"><a href="https://www.bclaws.gov.bc.ca/civix/document/id/complete/statreg/8_2026" target="_blank" rel="noopener">B.C. Reg. 8/2026</a> <a href="https://app.bchydro.com/accounts-billing/electrical-connections/large-load/emerging-industries-connections.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BC Hydro</a></span></p>
            </details>
            <details>
              <summary>Why email instead of signing a petition?</summary>
              <p>An email from someone in the riding, with an address, goes straight to the MLA’s office and shows a real constituent cares. A petition name is one of thousands.</p>
            </details>
            <details>
              <summary>Who’s behind this?</summary>
              <p>Just me, Nicholas Rempel. I’m a British Columbian who pays a BC Hydro bill, and I built this on my own time. I work in tech, so I know what data centres need, and I think they should pay for it. No data centre company, utility or political party funds this, and nobody pays me to do it. Questions or corrections: <a href="mailto:hello@growthegrid.ca">hello@growthegrid.ca</a>.</p>
            </details>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
